docs/reviews\frame-theater-grades-6-to-8-codex-2026-11-25.md.log:4292:docs/courses/theater-grades-9-to-12/frame.md:277:| # | Year | Unit and compelling question | Days | Skills | Owned standards | Processes, anchors and works | What students learn | Why this unit, here | Check set adds | Sensitive topics and teacher notes |
docs/reviews\frame-theater-grades-6-to-8-codex-2026-11-25.md.log:4293:docs/courses/theater-grades-9-to-12/frame.md:279:| 1 | Level 1 | Bridge: back to the stage. *What does a company need to know before it can rehearse a play together?* | 12 | | None | Performing (Pr4 to Pr6, as readiness); responding (Re7) as review. Works: a Curio Class original two-person scene, rehearsed; Lady Gregory, Spreading the News (Ireland, 1904, first staged at the Abbey Theatre, Dublin; public domain), one page read aloud for the story map | Bridge days, readiness only (STANDARD.md 3.2; no id claimed, counted in the bar's 10 percent): the warm-up routine (body, breath, voice); stage areas, blocking, cheating out and levels; script markup (beats, stage directions); objective, obstacle and tactic; the story map; the critique protocol (describe, ask, suggest); group agreements and the rehearsal log; a prop sketch and a three-cue sheet; the audience journal, look-describe-interpret and "because" with a detail; the two-ties card. Readiness check: last ten minutes of day 1. Words: stage left, downstage, blocking, beat, objective, cue. Showing: the original scene in pairs for the class. No end-of-unit assessment (no id): a readiness record only, met, partly or not yet on each technique, which sets the support in units 2 to 5. Tools: step cards, the original scene, a stage-areas floor card, Curio Class's own. Free path: tape spike marks, chairs. Short path: 6 days | First because every Level 1 unit uses these techniques and models, and Level 1 assumes no earlier theater (lesson 32: theater 6 to 8 is not core). 12 days, the most the bridge may take | Candidate: the bridge's readiness check written in the same words as sequence-write.md rule 2 | Hazards and controls: desks and chairs to the walls, a clear floor, soft soles or bare feet; warm-ups with room for arms out; no lifts or falls; partner contact by agreement only. Agent reads: sensitivity; safety (hands-on). |
docs/reviews\frame-theater-grades-6-to-8-codex-2026-11-25.md.log:4294:docs/courses/theater-grades-9-to-12/frame.md:280:| 2 | Level 1 | Where a play comes from. *How do relationships between characters drive a play, and how do a play's roots shape how a company could stage it?* | 34 | th-g9-story; th-g9-imagine | TH:Pr4.1.Ia; TH:Cn11.2.Ia; TH:Cn11.2.Ib; TH:Cr1.1.Ia; TH:Cr2.1.Ia | Performing (Pr4); connecting (Cn11); creating (Cr1, Cr2). Works: Lady Gregory, Spreading the News (Ireland, 1904, public domain), staged in excerpts; Hanay Geiogamah (Kiowa and Delaware) and the American Indian Theatre Ensemble (founded 1972 at La MaMa, New York), plays built by a Native company from its members' storytelling, from a university's or the company's page, described and linked, never copied; the Abbey Theatre's own history pages on its founding (1904) | Retrieval in day 2's warm-up: script markup and the story map (bridge). TH:Pr4.1.Ia: map how the relationships between characters move the story of Spreading the News (who needs whom, who misreads whom), on a relationship map. TH:Cn11.2.Ia: research how theater artists tell stories, comparing the Abbey's scripted comedy with the ensemble's plays built from its members' storytelling. TH:Cn11.2.Ib: simple research on the play's cultural and social roots (the Irish literary revival, a small town's gossip, the Abbey's purpose), from two sources Curio Class writes and one the student finds. TH:Cr1.1.Ia: research to form ideas for how a production of the play could look (place, period, light). TH:Cr2.1.Ia: test in a concept page how history and culture shape the concept, analyzing the student's own first ideas against the research. Words: relationship, root, source, concept, research, period. Showing: a staged excerpt with its relationship map for the class. Mid-unit assessment (around unit day 17): the research note and staged excerpt (th-g9-story). End-of-unit evidence, id by id: TH:Pr4.1.Ia, a look-for on the staged excerpt: the relationship that turns the scene made visible in the blocking; TH:Cn11.2.Ia, a sitting item: an unseen description of a devised scene, how its makers told the story compared with a scripted one; TH:Cn11.2.Ib, a kept research note with two sources on the play's roots, scored after the session; TH:Cr1.1.Ia, a guide look-for on the concept page: a stated look for a production, tied to research; TH:Cr2.1.Ia, a guide look-for on the same page: one way history or culture changed the student's first idea, with the reason. Each item is marked met, partly or not yet. Out: a team interpretation (Level 2), directors' styles (Level 3). Tools: relationship map, concept page, research note frame, Curio Class's own; Curio Class writes the two root readings. Free path: printed excerpts, pencils. Short path: 17 days | After the bridge because research and concept need script markup and the story map; first of the paired units because every later Level 1 unit uses a researched reading of a play (the character unit's given circumstances, the design unit's concept). Two rows on research and story pair naturally: the roots feed the concept | Candidate: a third storytelling tradition from Asia or Africa beside the Abbey and the ensemble, described and linked | The American Indian Theatre Ensemble: the sensitivity agent reads the company's or a Native-led university program's own pages first; the class studies how the plays were built, and no student performs or imitates a Native story, song or ceremony. Spreading the News is a comedy of gossip; no student is cast to type. Hazards and controls: a clear floor for staging; no real weapons (the play's "murder" is a misunderstanding, nothing is mimed as violence). Agent reads: sensitivity; safety (hands-on). |
docs/reviews\frame-theater-grades-6-to-8-codex-2026-11-25.md.log:4295:docs/courses/theater-grades-9-to-12/frame.md:281:| 3 | Level 1 | Inside a character. *How does an actor build a believable person from what the script gives, and how do our own lives shape what we see in a performance?* | 34 | th-g9-actor; th-g9-notice | TH:Pr4.1.Ib; TH:Pr5.1.Ia; TH:Cr3.1.Ib; TH:Cr1.1.Ic; TH:Re7.1.Ia; TH:Re8.1.Ia; TH:Re8.1.Ic | Performing (Pr4, Pr5); creating (Cr1, Cr3); responding (Re7, Re8). Works: Alice Gerstenberg, Overtones (United States, 1913; public domain), a one-act in which two women's hidden selves are played by two more actors, scenes rehearsed; Michael Chekhov's psychological gesture (Russia and the United States, To the Actor, 1953; Chekhov 1891 to 1955), in Curio Class's own summary; Anna Deavere Smith, Twilight: Los Angeles, 1992 (United States, first staged 1993; born 1950, the unit plan records whether living, as of its date), one performer shaping many real people from interviews, described and linked | Retrieval in day 2's warm-up: the warm-up routine and objective, obstacle and tactic (bridge). TH:Pr4.1.Ib: let the given circumstances found in the script (who, where, when, what just happened) guide how the student builds the character. TH:Pr5.1.Ia: work on varied acting techniques (an objective-led run, a psychological gesture, a vocal-pitch exercise) to widen the student's range. TH:Cr3.1.Ib: try body, voice and physical-response choices and keep the most believable. TH:Cr1.1.Ic: keep script notes on the character (wants, fears, what others say of them) as ideas for a believable character. TH:Re7.1.Ia: from what was seen and heard in a scene, draft criteria for judging artists' choices. TH:Re8.1.Ia: compare choices artists made from personal experience across several works (Gerstenberg's hidden selves, Michael Chekhov's gesture, Smith's interviews). TH:Re8.1.Ic: justify the student's own taste and beliefs after taking part in and watching a scene, with a public or invented viewpoint offered beside the student's own and marked the same. Words: given circumstances, objective, tactic, believable, criteria, taste. Showing: a performed character study in pairs. Mid-unit assessment (around unit day 18): the performed character study with its rehearsal log (th-g9-actor). End-of-unit evidence (two check days), id by id: TH:Pr4.1.Ib, a look-for on the character study: choices that follow the given circumstances; TH:Pr5.1.Ia, a kept rehearsal log naming two techniques tried and what each changed; TH:Cr3.1.Ib, a look-for: one body and one voice choice that make the character believable; TH:Cr1.1.Ic, the kept script notes; TH:Re7.1.Ia, a sitting item: criteria drafted from an unseen teacher demonstration; TH:Re8.1.Ia, a sitting item: two artists' choices compared, each tied to the artist's experience; TH:Re8.1.Ic, a kept audience-journal entry justifying a taste after a classmate's showing. Each item is marked met, partly or not yet. Out: a character file with the director's vision (Level 2). Tools: character map, rehearsal log, audience journal, Curio Class's own. Free path: printed scenes. Short path: 17 days | After unit 2 because building a character uses the researched reading of a script; before the design unit because design serves characters already understood. Criteria are formed here, from the student's own response, so unit 4's critique has criteria to use | Candidate: a fourth work from Asia or Africa on how an actor prepares, in a practitioner's own words | Twilight: Los Angeles concerns the 1992 unrest after a police beating: the unit studies how Smith shaped the work, not the events in depth; the sensitivity agent reads the performer's own and a theater's pages first, and no student performs a real person from the play or by an accent. Personal beliefs are invited, never required: a public or invented viewpoint is offered and marked the same. Overtones turns on two women's rivalry over a man: played as written, no student cast by appearance. Health's steps if a student tells something. Agent reads: sensitivity; safety (hands-on). |
docs/reviews\frame-theater-grades-6-to-8-codex-2026-11-25.md.log:4296:docs/courses/theater-grades-9-to-12/frame.md:282:| 4 | Level 1 | Design and the critic. *How does a technology change what a designer can do, and how do we judge a play fairly?* | 34 | th-g9-design; th-g9-judge | TH:Cr1.1.Ib; TH:Pr5.1.Ib; TH:Cr3.1.Ic; TH:Re9.1.Ib; TH:Re9.1.Ia; TH:Re9.1.Ic | Creating (Cr1, Cr3); performing (Pr5); responding (Re9). Works: Josef Svoboda's Laterna Magika (Prague, 1958; Svoboda 1920 to 2002), live actors joined with projected film, from the Prague company's or a museum's page; Jean Rosenthal's lighting for dance and Broadway (United States; Rosenthal 1912 to 1969), from a library's archive page; Tomson Highway (Cree, born 1951 in northern Manitoba; the unit plan records whether living, as of its date), The Rez Sisters (Toronto, 1986), seven women of a reserve planning a trip to the biggest bingo in the world, described and linked | Retrieval in day 2's warm-up: the prop sketch and cue sheet (bridge). TH:Cr1.1.Ib: research how one technology (projected film, the electric lighting board) changed design choices. TH:Pr5.1.Ib: use the researched element in a design for a scene of unit 2's or 3's play (a light plot from flashlights and cellophane, or a shadow projection made with a flashlight and a cut card). TH:Cr3.1.Ic: refine the design in a run so it serves the story and its mood. TH:Re9.1.Ib: reflect on how a show's production elements look and feel. TH:Re9.1.Ia: analyze a drama work with evidence and criteria, tied to other arts, history and culture (The Rez Sisters' reserve life, its comedy and the trickster figure in it). TH:Re9.1.Ic: weigh a play's purpose and intended audience to deepen appreciation. Words: design, technology, plot, cue, production element, purpose. Showing: the design shown in a run of the scene for the class. Mid-unit assessment (around unit day 18): the design folio with its short written defense (th-g9-design). End-of-unit evidence (two check days), id by id: TH:Cr1.1.Ib, the kept research page on one technology; TH:Pr5.1.Ib, a look-for on the run: the researched element used in the design; TH:Cr3.1.Ic, a look-for: one change after the critique that strengthens the mood; TH:Re9.1.Ib, a sitting item: how the elements of an unseen described staging look and feel; TH:Re9.1.Ia, the kept written critique of The Rez Sisters with evidence, criteria and one tie to history or another art, drafted, given feedback and revised; TH:Re9.1.Ic, a sitting item: the purpose and intended audience of an unseen scene. Each item is marked met, partly or not yet. Out: a design that carries a concept (Level 2). Tools: cue-sheet practice tool or its printed sheet, design folio, criteria card, Curio Class's own; Curio Class writes the Svoboda and Rosenthal readings. Free path: LED flashlights, cellophane, card. Short path: 17 days | After the character unit because design serves characters and story already studied; the critique uses unit 3's criteria. Before unit 5 because the production needs a design and a habit of fair judgment | Candidate: a living designer from Africa or Latin America, described and linked | The Rez Sisters: the sensitivity agent reads the playwright's own words and a Native-led theater's page first; the play's trickster, Nanabush, is a figure of Cree and Ojibwe story, studied in the playwright's own telling and never performed by students; its scene of sexual violence is not read or staged. Projections: no flashing images. Hazards and controls: LED flashlights only, no light into eyes, no flashing; card cut by an adult with a blade; no work at height; no school stage lights touched by students. Agent reads: sensitivity; safety (hands-on). |
docs/reviews\frame-theater-grades-6-to-8-codex-2026-11-25.md.log:4297:docs/courses/theater-grades-9-to-12/frame.md:283:| 5 | Level 1 | The company and its town. *How do the beliefs of a time and place shape a play, and how does a company bring a play to the audience it chooses?* | 42 | th-g9-world; th-g9-show | TH:Cn10.1.Ia; TH:Cn11.1.Ia; TH:Re8.1.Ib; TH:Cr2.1.Ib; TH:Cr3.1.Ia; TH:Pr6.1.Ia | Connecting (Cn10, Cn11); responding (Re8); creating (Cr2, Cr3); performing (Pr6). Works: Henrik Ibsen, An Enemy of the People (Norway, 1882, R. Farquharson Sharp's translation, 1911; public domain), staged in scenes; Satyajit Ray, Ganashatru (India, 1989, the play moved to a Bengali town), described and linked; Wole Soyinka, The Lion and the Jewel (Nigeria, 1959; born 1934, the unit plan records whether living, as of its date), old and new beliefs in a Yoruba village, described and linked | Retrieval in day 2's warm-up: group agreements, the rehearsal log and the two-ties card (bridge). TH:Cn10.1.Ia: investigate how culture, local ideas and the student's own beliefs (or a public or invented viewpoint, marked the same) shape An Enemy of the People. TH:Cn11.1.Ia: explore how belief systems of other cultures, places and eras shaped similar choices (Ray's Bengal, Soyinka's village). TH:Re8.1.Ib: compare cultural views and settings that could change how the play is judged. TH:Cr2.1.Ib: map how designers, playwrights, directors and actors depend on one another in a production. TH:Cr3.1.Ia: rehearse and rework the scenes with standard staging conventions. TH:Pr6.1.Ia: perform the scenes for an audience the class chooses (another class, families, a community group). Words: dramaturg, belief, setting, convention, role, audience. Showing: the class production of scenes from An Enemy of the People, with the artist statement. Mid-unit assessment (around unit day 18): the dramaturgy page and class discussion (th-g9-world). End-of-unit evidence (two check days), id by id: TH:Cn10.1.Ia, a guide look-for on the dramaturgy page: one cultural, one local and one belief tie to the play; TH:Cn11.1.Ia, a look-for on the same page: a belief system of another place or era and the choice it shaped; TH:Re8.1.Ib, a kept discussion note: two cultural views that could change how the play is judged; TH:Cr2.1.Ib, a kept roles map with each role's dependence named; TH:Cr3.1.Ia, a look-for on the production: staging conventions used and one change from rehearsal; TH:Pr6.1.Ia, a look-for on the production: the scene played for the chosen audience. Each item is marked met, partly or not yet. The production is the unit's showing day; the rehearsal record and roles map are scored after the session. Out: a team interpretation (Level 2). Tools: dramaturgy page, roles map, artist statement, Curio Class's own. Free path: printed scenes, found costume pieces. Short path: 19 days | Last in Level 1 because the production uses every earlier unit (research, character, design, criteria) and ends the level; the artist-statement model starts here, where it is first needed. The year-end check follows in week 32 | Candidate: the production's audience and a family yes for any showing outside class, set in the unit plan | Personal beliefs are invited, never required: a public or invented viewpoint is offered beside the student's own and marked the same. The Lion and the Jewel: the sensitivity agent reads Yoruba-led and Nigerian theater sources first; the play's bride-price plot is studied, not performed, and no student plays a Yoruba character by an accent. Ganashatru concerns a town's poisoned water and religion: the agent reads the film's archive page first. A showing outside class needs the family's yes, or the student's own at 18 or over. Hazards and controls: a clear floor; set pieces at floor level only; no climbing. Agent reads: sensitivity; safety (hands-on). |
docs/reviews\frame-theater-grades-6-to-8-codex-2026-11-25.md.log:4298:docs/courses/theater-grades-9-to-12/frame.md:284:| 6 | Level 2 | Review and placement. *What does a Level 1 company already know how to do?* | 6 | | None | Creating, performing and responding, as review. Works: a Curio Class original scene for three actors; one scene of Level 1's An Enemy of the People revisited | Review days, inside the standards (STANDARD.md 3.2): retrieval of every Level 1 thread (concept page, character study, design folio, written critique, dramaturgy page, a staged scene). The placement: a student new to Level 2 shows work scored against Level 1's year-end check, and starts Level 2 if it meets it. Words: review, placement, level. Showing: the original scene in groups. No end-of-unit assessment of its own (no id): the placement record, met, partly or not yet on Level 1's sampled ids. Short path: 3 days | First in Level 2 because every Level 2 row assumes its Level 1 row (one level back); it doubles as placement | Candidate: the placement task's sample at two levels | Hazards and controls: as unit 1. Agent reads: sensitivity; safety (hands-on). |
docs/reviews\frame-theater-grades-6-to-8-codex-2026-11-25.md.log:4299:docs/courses/theater-grades-9-to-12/frame.md:285:| 7 | Level 2 | Customs and lasting plays. *Why do some plays last for centuries, and how do a culture's customs shape the way a play looks?* | 35 | th-g10-story; th-g10-imagine | TH:Pr4.1.IIa; TH:Cn11.2.IIa; TH:Cn11.2.IIb; TH:Cr1.1.IIa; TH:Cr2.1.IIa | Performing (Pr4); connecting (Cn11); creating (Cr1, Cr2). Works: Noh as Zeami Motokiyo shaped it (Japan, fourteenth and fifteenth centuries; Noh on UNESCO's list since 2008), its masks, painted pine and bridgeway studied by looking only, from UNESCO's page and a Japanese Noh institution's page; Hagoromo (The Feather Mantle), a Noh play in Arthur Waley's translation (The Noh Plays of Japan, 1921; public domain), rehearsed as text in the class's own staging, never in Noh form; a living director's staging of a classic, picked by the student from a list the unit plan gives | Retrieval in day 2's warm-up: unit 2's relationship map and concept page (th-g9-story, th-g9-imagine). TH:Pr4.1.IIa: find what distinctive choices make Hagoromo believable and lasting after six centuries. TH:Cn11.2.IIa: let research on a topic the student picks (the play's pine shore, its feather robe, its staging rules) shape the student's creative choices. TH:Cn11.2.IIb: check how the student's own biases and beliefs may skew the research findings, with a bias check on the research note. TH:Cr1.1.IIa: look into how past and cultural customs (Noh's masks and bare stage, and a second custom of the student's choosing) shape a production's look. TH:Cr2.1.IIa: sharpen a concept page, showing which customs shaped it. Words: lasting, distinctive, custom, convention, bias, concept. Showing: a staged excerpt with its concept page. Mid-unit assessment (around unit day 17): the research note with its bias check and a staged excerpt (th-g10-story). End-of-unit evidence (two check days), id by id: TH:Pr4.1.IIa, a sitting item: the distinctive choices that keep an unseen described classic scene alive; TH:Cn11.2.IIa, a look-for on the excerpt: a staging choice traced to the student's research; TH:Cn11.2.IIb, the kept bias check: one belief that could skew a finding and how the student tested it; TH:Cr1.1.IIa, a look-for on the concept page: two customs that shape the look; TH:Cr2.1.IIa, a look-for: the concept sharpened after feedback, with the change named. Each item is marked met, partly or not yet. Out: blending forms and traditions (Level 3). Tools: research note, bias check card, concept page, Curio Class's own; Curio Class writes the Noh readings. Free path: printed scenes. Short path: 17 days | First of Level 2's paired units, in the same slot as unit 2, because every later Level 2 unit uses the sharpened concept and checked research; review in unit 6 retrieves Level 1's research | Candidate: a Native American or African classic told in its community's own published words, for range | Noh is a living tradition with Buddhist roots: the sensitivity agent reads UNESCO's page and a Noh institution's own pages first; masks are studied by looking only, never made or worn as Noh masks, and no student imitates Noh movement, chant or dance; Okina, a ritual piece, is not used. Agent reads: sensitivity; safety (hands-on). |
docs/reviews\frame-theater-grades-6-to-8-codex-2026-11-25.md.log:4300:docs/courses/theater-grades-9-to-12/frame.md:286:| 8 | Level 2 | The actor's file. *How do script clues, research and a director's vision shape a character, and why do audiences disagree about the same performance?* | 35 | th-g10-actor; th-g10-notice | TH:Pr4.1.IIb; TH:Pr5.1.IIa; TH:Cr3.1.IIb; TH:Cr1.1.IIc; TH:Re7.1.IIa; TH:Re8.1.IIa; TH:Re8.1.IIc | Performing (Pr4, Pr5); creating (Cr1, Cr3); responding (Re7, Re8). Works: Molière, The Miser (France, 1668, Charles Heron Wall's translation, 1870s; public domain), scenes rehearsed; Plautus, The Pot of Gold (Aulularia, Rome, about 200 BCE, in a public domain translation), the comedy Molière's miser came from, studied in excerpts; Lynn Nottage, Sweat (United States, 2015, Pulitzer Prize 2017; the unit plan records whether living, as of its date), described and linked | Retrieval in day 2's warm-up: unit 3's character map and audience-journal criteria (th-g9-actor, th-g9-notice). TH:Pr4.1.IIb: name the script details, research and director's vision behind each of the character's choices, in a character file. TH:Pr5.1.IIa: sharpen varied acting skills (status, tempo, the miser's physical center) for a believable, lasting performance. TH:Cr3.1.IIb: revise body and voice choices from the file's research and script study. TH:Cr1.1.IIc: draw on the student's own experience and knowledge, or an invented person's, for a believable character. TH:Re7.1.IIa: show how two readings of the class's criteria would steer different next choices. TH:Re8.1.IIa: build proof and standards behind artistic choices. TH:Re8.1.IIc: debate and sort the range of tastes and beliefs in responses to Sweat and to the class's scenes. Words: character file, status, vision, reading, proof, debate. Showing: a performed character study. Mid-unit assessment (around unit day 17): the character study with its rehearsal log (th-g10-actor). End-of-unit evidence (two check days), id by id: TH:Pr4.1.IIb, the kept character file; TH:Pr5.1.IIa, a look-for: two acting skills used for a believable performance; TH:Cr3.1.IIb, a look-for: a revised choice traced to research or script study; TH:Cr1.1.IIc, a look-for on the file: a choice drawn from experience or knowledge (the student's own or an invented person's, marked the same); TH:Re7.1.IIa, a sitting item: two readings of a criterion applied to an unseen scene, with the next choice each would steer; TH:Re8.1.IIa, a kept response with proof and a stated standard; TH:Re8.1.IIc, a sitting item: three tastes in a set of unseen audience responses sorted and debated. Each item is marked met, partly or not yet. Out: defended techniques from sources (Level 3). Tools: character file, audience journal, Curio Class's own. Free path: printed scenes, found props. Short path: 17 days | Same slot as unit 3; after unit 7 because the character file uses checked research and a concept; before unit 9, whose critique uses the standards built here | Candidate: a character-comedy tradition from Asia or Africa beside Plautus and Molière | Sweat: a factory town's job losses, drug use and a violent assault; studied from a description of its choices, never performed, with a content note to students and families; the sensitivity agent reads the playwright's and a theater's pages first. Personal experience is invited, never required. Health's steps if a student tells something. Agent reads: sensitivity; safety (hands-on). |
docs/reviews\frame-theater-grades-6-to-8-codex-2026-11-25.md.log:4301:docs/courses/theater-grades-9-to-12/frame.md:287:| 9 | Level 2 | A design with a concept. *How can a design carry a production's idea, and how well does a play reach the audience it was made for?* | 35 | th-g10-design; th-g10-judge | TH:Cr1.1.IIb; TH:Pr5.1.IIb; TH:Cr3.1.IIc; TH:Re9.1.IIb; TH:Re9.1.IIa; TH:Re9.1.IIc | Creating (Cr1, Cr3); performing (Pr5); responding (Re9). Works: Ming Cho Lee's set designs (born in Shanghai, China, 1930, designed in the United States, died 2020), from a museum's or a university archive's page; Julie Taymor's masks and puppets for The Lion King (United States, 1997; the unit plan records whether living, as of its date), described and linked; Madeline Sayet (Mohegan; the unit plan records whether living, as of its date), Where We Belong (first staged 2019, at Shakespeare's Globe, London), a solo play about a Mohegan woman's journey to England and to Shakespeare, described and linked | Retrieval in day 2's warm-up: unit 4's design folio and criteria card (th-g9-design, th-g9-judge). TH:Cr1.1.IIb: solve a design problem (a scene that must change place in seconds) with what the student knows of stage technology. TH:Pr5.1.IIb: design a production that carries a concept, with researched elements. TH:Cr3.1.IIc: rethink and redo the design in rehearsal to strengthen the story's feeling. TH:Re9.1.IIb: make meaning of a play from taste and production knowledge while respecting others' readings. TH:Re9.1.IIa: weigh and judge Where We Belong by tying it to other arts, history and culture. TH:Re9.1.IIc: check how it reaches its intended audience and purpose. Words: concept, convey, transition, reading, purpose, solo. Showing: the design in a run of a scene. Mid-unit assessment (around unit day 17): the design folio with its written defense (th-g10-design). End-of-unit evidence (two check days), id by id: TH:Cr1.1.IIb, a look-for on the folio: the design problem and its technical solution; TH:Pr5.1.IIb, a look-for on the run: the design carrying the stated concept; TH:Cr3.1.IIc, a look-for: one redone choice after rehearsal; TH:Re9.1.IIb, a sitting item: a reading of an unseen described staging, with a classmate's other reading answered fairly; TH:Re9.1.IIa, the kept written critique tying the play to history, culture and another art, drafted, given feedback and revised; TH:Re9.1.IIc, a sitting item: how an unseen scene reaches its intended audience. Each item is marked met, partly or not yet. Out: a full design defended (Level 3). Tools: design folio, cue-sheet practice tool or its printed sheet, criteria card, Curio Class's own. Free path: card models, flashlights, cellophane. Short path: 17 days | Same slot as unit 4; after unit 8 because the concept-led design serves characters already filed; before unit 10's team production | Candidate: a designer from Africa or Latin America, described and linked | Where We Belong: the sensitivity agent reads the playwright's own words and the Mohegan Tribe's own published history first; the class studies the play's choices, and no student performs it or plays a Mohegan character. Taymor's masks are studied, not copied as a culture's dress. Hazards and controls: as unit 4. Agent reads: sensitivity; safety (hands-on). |
docs/reviews\frame-theater-grades-6-to-8-codex-2026-11-25.md.log:4302:docs/courses/theater-grades-9-to-12/frame.md:288:| 10 | Level 2 | A play that mirrors or challenges. *How can a company blend arts and traditions into one piece, and what does a play teach us about a culture?* | 45 | th-g10-world; th-g10-show | TH:Cn10.1.IIa; TH:Cn11.1.IIa; TH:Re8.1.IIb; TH:Cr2.1.IIb; TH:Cr3.1.IIa; TH:Pr6.1.IIa | Connecting (Cn10, Cn11); responding (Re8); creating (Cr2, Cr3); performing (Pr6). Works: Ama Ata Aidoo, The Dilemma of a Ghost (Ghana, first staged 1964; Aidoo 1940 to 2023), a Ghanaian man brings his African American wife home, with song and a folk-tale frame, described and linked; the Philippine Educational Theater Association (Manila, founded 1967), plays that blend song, dance and local languages, from the company's own page; the play the class picks to stage, public domain or student-written | Retrieval in day 2's warm-up: unit 5's dramaturgy page and roles map (th-g9-world, th-g9-show). TH:Cn10.1.IIa: pick and interpret a play that mirrors or challenges the student's beliefs (or a public figure's, marked the same). TH:Cn11.1.IIa: blend practices from other arts and subjects (song, storytelling, a social studies source) into a cross-cultural piece, made in groups (an application day, scored as making). TH:Re8.1.IIb: use a drama work (The Dilemma of a Ghost) to gain insight into a culture, from Ghanaian sources. TH:Cr2.1.IIb: agree as a team on how to interpret the play. TH:Cr3.1.IIa: use rehearsal to test the concept and the tech design. TH:Pr6.1.IIa: present for a chosen audience, in a way the process shaped. Words: interpret, mirror, blend, cross-cultural, team, process. Showing: the class production with its artist statement. Mid-unit assessment (around unit day 18): the written reflection on the chosen play and the cross-cultural piece (th-g10-world). End-of-unit evidence (two check days), id by id: TH:Cn10.1.IIa, the kept reflection: the play's link to beliefs, with evidence; TH:Cn11.1.IIa, a look-for on the cross-cultural piece: two practices blended, each named with its source; TH:Re8.1.IIb, a sitting item: one insight into a culture from an unseen described scene, with its evidence; TH:Cr2.1.IIb, the kept team interpretation sheet; TH:Cr3.1.IIa, a look-for on the production: a concept or design change made from rehearsal; TH:Pr6.1.IIa, a look-for: the production shaped for its chosen audience. Each item is marked met, partly or not yet. Out: devising on a global issue (Level 3). Tools: team interpretation sheet, artist statement, Curio Class's own. Free path: printed scripts, found costume pieces. Short path: 22 days | Last in Level 2, same slot as unit 5, because the production draws on every earlier Level 2 unit and ends the level; the year-end check follows in week 32 | Candidate: a Native American storytelling-theater company's own teaching, for range | The Dilemma of a Ghost: the sensitivity agent reads Aidoo's own published words and Ghanaian theater sources first; the play's family and its talk of childlessness are studied, not performed, and no student plays a Ghanaian or African American character by an accent. The company's blended forms are studied in its own words; no student imitates a Philippine dance or song. Beliefs: a public or invented option marked the same. A showing outside class needs a yes. Hazards and controls: as unit 5. Agent reads: sensitivity; safety (hands-on). |
docs/reviews\frame-theater-grades-6-to-8-codex-2026-11-25.md.log:4303:docs/courses/theater-grades-9-to-12/frame.md:289:| 11 | Level 3 | Review and placement. *What does an Accomplished company bring to Advanced work?* | 6 | | None | Creating, performing and responding, as review. Works: a Curio Class original scene with an open ending; one scene of a Level 2 play revisited | Review days, inside the standards: retrieval of every Level 2 thread. Placement: a student new to Level 3 shows work scored against Level 2's year-end check. Words: review, placement, advanced. Showing: the original scene with endings the groups choose. Placement record only (no id). Short path: 3 days | First in Level 3 because every Level 3 row assumes its Level 2 row; doubles as placement | Candidate: the placement task's sample at two levels | Hazards and controls: as unit 1. Agent reads: sensitivity; safety (hands-on). |
docs/reviews\frame-theater-grades-6-to-8-codex-2026-11-25.md.log:4304:docs/courses/theater-grades-9-to-12/frame.md:290:| 12 | Level 3 | The director's concept. *How do directors build a concept, and when does borrowing a tradition become taking it?* | 35 | th-g11-story; th-g11-imagine | TH:Pr4.1.IIIa; TH:Cn11.2.IIIa; TH:Cn11.2.IIIb; TH:Cr1.1.IIIa; TH:Cr2.1.IIIa | Performing (Pr4); connecting (Cn11); creating (Cr1, Cr2). Works: Yukio Ninagawa, Ninagawa Macbeth (Japan, Tokyo, 1980; Ninagawa 1935 to 2016), Shakespeare moved to samurai-era Japan, described and linked; Ariane Mnouchkine and the Théâtre du Soleil, Les Atrides (France, 1990 to 1992; Mnouchkine born 1939, the unit plan records whether living, as of its date), Greek tragedy staged with forms drawn from Kathakali and Kabuki, described and linked, with published criticism of that borrowing; Shakespeare's Macbeth (England, about 1606; public domain), scenes staged | Retrieval in day 2's warm-up: unit 7's concept page and bias check (th-g10-imagine, th-g10-story). TH:Pr4.1.IIIa: research two directors' styles and shape the student's own directing concept for a Macbeth scene. TH:Cn11.2.IIIa: defend creative choices by reading the research critically (who wrote it, from where, what it leaves out). TH:Cn11.2.IIIb: defend a view of Macbeth's social and historical meaning, in a director's note. TH:Cr1.1.IIIa: blend dramatic forms, conventions and technology into a production look. TH:Cr2.1.IIIa: build an original play idea from analysis, research and traditions from more than one part of the world, crediting each, never imitating a tradition's sacred form. Words: directing concept, style, convention, appropriation, critical, original. Showing: a directed excerpt with the director's note. Mid-unit assessment (around unit day 17): the director's note and a staged excerpt (th-g11-story). End-of-unit evidence (two check days), id by id: TH:Pr4.1.IIIa, a look-for on the excerpt: a concept traced to two directors' styles; TH:Cn11.2.IIIa, the kept source critique behind two choices; TH:Cn11.2.IIIb, the kept director's note defending a social and historical reading; TH:Cr1.1.IIIa, a look-for on the concept portfolio: forms, conventions and technology blended; TH:Cr2.1.IIIa, a look-for: an original play idea with its research and two credited traditions, and a sitting item: whether an unseen described production borrows fairly, with reasons. Each item is marked met, partly or not yet. Out: the student's own production concept (Level 4). Tools: concept portfolio, source critique card, Curio Class's own. Free path: printed scenes. Short path: 17 days | Same slot as units 2 and 7; Advanced work starts from a concept the student sets, which every later Level 3 unit uses | Candidate: a director from Africa or Latin America as the second style, for range | Macbeth's killings are spoken, never staged as contact or with any weapon (mimed or a word). Kathakali and Kabuki: the sensitivity agent reads UNESCO's Kabuki page, a Kerala Kathakali institution's own page and published criticism of Les Atrides first; no student wears makeup, mask or costume of either tradition. The borrowing question is studied with care for the communities named. Agent reads: sensitivity; safety (hands-on). |
docs/reviews\frame-theater-grades-6-to-8-codex-2026-11-25.md.log:4305:docs/courses/theater-grades-9-to-12/frame.md:291:| 13 | Level 3 | Technique and sources. *Which acting techniques serve a role, and how do we know a source is sound?* | 35 | th-g11-actor; th-g11-notice | TH:Pr4.1.IIIb; TH:Pr5.1.IIIa; TH:Cr3.1.IIIb; TH:Cr1.1.IIIc; TH:Re7.1.IIIa; TH:Re8.1.IIIa; TH:Re8.1.IIIc | Performing (Pr4, Pr5); creating (Cr1, Cr3); responding (Re7, Re8). Works: Aphra Behn, The Rover (England, 1677; public domain), scenes rehearsed; Tadashi Suzuki's method of actor training (Japan, Toga; born 1939, the unit plan records whether living, as of its date), from his company's page; the Viewpoints of Anne Bogart and Tina Landau (United States, The Viewpoints Book, 2005), in Curio Class's own summary; Michael Chekhov's gesture from unit 3, revisited | Retrieval in day 2's warm-up: unit 8's character file and proof and standards (th-g10-actor, th-g10-notice). TH:Pr4.1.IIIb: use several researched techniques (objectives, Viewpoints, a Suzuki-based stance exercise taught from the company's published teaching) to guide character choices. TH:Pr5.1.IIIa: pick and defend warm-ups and exercises from sound sources. TH:Cr3.1.IIIb: pull research, script study and context into one believable performance. TH:Cr1.1.IIIc: weave history, culture and the student's own experience (or an invented person's) into the character. TH:Re7.1.IIIa: use history and culture to organize and defend a response to a play. TH:Re8.1.IIIa: use proof and fitting standards to revise the student's own work and interpret others'. TH:Re8.1.IIIc: explain the student's tastes and beliefs as grounds for the research behind choices. Words: technique, source, sound, context, ground, revise. Showing: a performed role from The Rover. Mid-unit assessment (around unit day 17): the performed character study with a technique log (th-g11-actor). End-of-unit evidence (two check days), id by id: TH:Pr4.1.IIIb, a look-for: two researched techniques visible in the choices; TH:Pr5.1.IIIa, the kept technique log with each exercise's source and why it is sound; TH:Cr3.1.IIIb, a look-for: research, script and context joined in one believable performance; TH:Cr1.1.IIIc, a look-for on the log: a historical or cultural choice and a personal or invented one; TH:Re7.1.IIIa, the kept critique organized by the play's history and culture; TH:Re8.1.IIIa, a kept revision note: a change to the student's own work from proof and a standard; TH:Re8.1.IIIc, a sitting item: the student's taste stated as grounds for the research a choice needs, on an unseen scene. Each item is marked met, partly or not yet. Out: a self-chosen role (Level 4). Tools: technique log, critique frame, Curio Class's own. Free path: printed scenes. Short path: 17 days | Same slot as units 3 and 8; after unit 12 so the role serves a directing concept | Candidate: a West African or Latin American actor-training practice in its practitioner's own words | Suzuki training is taught only from the company's published teaching, at the students' own range, no forced stamping on a hard floor (soft soles, a short count). The Rover has a threatened assault in its plot: that scene is not staged; the unit picks scenes of wit and disguise, and the sensitivity agent reads the chosen scenes first. Personal experience is invited, never required. Hazards and controls: soft soles; no falls; swordplay in the script is spoken, never staged. Agent reads: sensitivity; safety (hands-on). |
docs/reviews\frame-theater-grades-6-to-8-codex-2026-11-25.md.log:4306:docs/courses/theater-grades-9-to-12/frame.md:292:| 14 | Level 3 | The full design and the current issue. *How does every element of a design serve one concept, and how does a play speak to issues its audience lives with now?* | 35 | th-g11-design; th-g11-judge | TH:Cr1.1.IIIb; TH:Pr5.1.IIIb; TH:Cr3.1.IIIc; TH:Re9.1.IIIb; TH:Re9.1.IIIa; TH:Re9.1.IIIc | Creating (Cr1, Cr3); performing (Pr5); responding (Re9). Works: Clint Ramos's set and costume designs (born in the Philippines, designs in the United States; the unit plan records whether living, as of its date), described and linked; Mary Kathryn Nagle (a citizen of the Cherokee Nation), Sovereignty (Arena Stage, Washington, 2018), described and linked; the design for the class's Macbeth or Rover scene | Retrieval in day 2's warm-up: unit 9's concept-led design and critique (th-g10-design, th-g10-judge). TH:Cr1.1.IIIb: make a full design for one scene using every element (set, light, sound, costume, props). TH:Pr5.1.IIIb: defend each element by how it carries the concept. TH:Cr3.1.IIIc: use advanced technical skills in rehearsal (a called cue sequence, a timed transition) to serve the story. TH:Re9.1.IIIb: weigh several aesthetic readings of one production's elements. TH:Re9.1.IIIa: use researched history and culture to back or challenge Sovereignty's choices. TH:Re9.1.IIIc: compare and debate how the play speaks to current issues for its audience (tribal sovereignty and a 2013 change in federal law that it stages). Words: full design, element, defend, aesthetic, sovereignty, current issue. Showing: the full design in a cued run. Mid-unit assessment (around unit day 17): the full design folio with its written defense (th-g11-design). End-of-unit evidence (two check days), id by id: TH:Cr1.1.IIIb, a look-for on the folio: every element designed; TH:Pr5.1.IIIb, the kept defense, element by element; TH:Cr3.1.IIIc, a look-for on the cued run: an advanced technical skill used to serve the story; TH:Re9.1.IIIb, a sitting item: two aesthetic readings of an unseen described staging weighed; TH:Re9.1.IIIa, the kept critique backing or challenging two of Sovereignty's choices with research, drafted, given feedback and revised; TH:Re9.1.IIIc, a sitting item: an unseen scene's tie to a current issue, debated in writing from two sides. Each item is marked met, partly or not yet. Out: the student's production design (Level 4). Tools: design folio, cue-sheet practice tool or its printed sheet, Curio Class's own. Free path: card models, flashlights, cellophane, found fabric. Short path: 17 days | Same slot as units 4 and 9; after unit 13 so the design serves a role and a concept built this level | Candidate: a designer from Africa or Latin America beside Ramos, for range | Sovereignty stages Cherokee removal, a political killing in 1839 and violence against a woman in the present day: studied from a description, never performed, with a content note to students and families; the sensitivity agent reads the playwright's own words, Arena Stage's page and the Cherokee Nation's own published history first. Leave-out: named on the course page. Hazards and controls: as unit 4; no student operates school stage lighting or rigging. Agent reads: sensitivity; safety (hands-on). |
