| 1 | Level 1 | Bridge: back to the stage. *What does a company need to know before it can rehearse a play together?* | 12 | | None | Performing (Pr4 to Pr6, as readiness); responding (Re7) as review. Works: a Curio Class original two-person scene, rehearsed; Lady Gregory, Spreading the News (Ireland, 1904, first staged at the Abbey Theatre, Dublin; public domain), one page read aloud for the story map | Bridge days, readiness only (STANDARD.md 3.2; no id claimed, counted in the bar's 10 percent): the warm-up routine (body, breath, voice); stage areas, blocking, cheating out and levels; script markup (beats, stage directions); objective, obstacle and tactic; the story map; the critique protocol (describe, ask, suggest); group agreements and the rehearsal log; a prop sketch and a three-cue sheet; the audience journal, look-describe-interpret and "because" with a detail; the two-ties card. Readiness check: last ten minutes of day 1. Words: stage left, downstage, blocking, beat, objective, cue. Showing: the original scene in pairs for the class. No end-of-unit assessment (no id): a readiness record only, met, partly or not yet on each technique, which sets the support in units 2 to 5. Tools: step cards, the original scene, a stage-areas floor card, Curio Class's own. Free path: tape spike marks, chairs. Short path: 6 days | First because every Level 1 unit uses these techniques and models, and Level 1 assumes no earlier theater (lesson 32: theater 6 to 8 is not core). 12 days, the most the bridge may take | Candidate: the bridge's readiness check written in the same words as sequence-write.md rule 2 | Hazards and controls: desks and chairs to the walls, a clear floor, soft soles or bare feet; warm-ups with room for arms out; no lifts or falls; partner contact by agreement only. Agent reads: sensitivity; safety (hands-on). |
| 2 | Level 1 | Where a play comes from. *How do relationships between characters drive a play, and how do a play's roots shape how a company could stage it?* | 34 | th-g9-story; th-g9-imagine | TH:Pr4.1.Ia; TH:Cn11.2.Ia; TH:Cn11.2.Ib; TH:Cr1.1.Ia; TH:Cr2.1.Ia | Performing (Pr4); connecting (Cn11); creating (Cr1, Cr2). Works: Lady Gregory, Spreading the News (Ireland, 1904, public domain), staged in excerpts; Hanay Geiogamah (Kiowa and Delaware) and the American Indian Theatre Ensemble (founded 1972 at La MaMa, New York), plays built by a Native company from its members' storytelling, from a university's or the company's page, described and linked, never copied; the Abbey Theatre's own history pages on its founding (1904) | Retrieval in day 2's warm-up: script markup and the story map (bridge). TH:Pr4.1.Ia: map how the relationships between characters move the story of Spreading the News (who needs whom, who misreads whom), on a relationship map. TH:Cn11.2.Ia: research how theater artists tell stories, comparing the Abbey's scripted comedy with the ensemble's plays built from its members' storytelling. TH:Cn11.2.Ib: simple research on the play's cultural and social roots (the Irish literary revival, a small town's gossip, the Abbey's purpose), from two sources Curio Class writes and one the student finds. TH:Cr1.1.Ia: research to form ideas for how a production of the play could look (place, period, light). TH:Cr2.1.Ia: test in a concept page how history and culture shape the concept, analyzing the student's own first ideas against the research. Words: relationship, root, source, concept, research, period. Showing: a staged excerpt with its relationship map for the class. Mid-unit assessment (around unit day 17): the research note and staged excerpt (th-g9-story). End-of-unit evidence, id by id: TH:Pr4.1.Ia, a look-for on the staged excerpt: the relationship that turns the scene made visible in the blocking; TH:Cn11.2.Ia, a sitting item: an unseen description of a devised scene, how its makers told the story compared with a scripted one; TH:Cn11.2.Ib, a kept research note with two sources on the play's roots, scored after the session; TH:Cr1.1.Ia, a guide look-for on the concept page: a stated look for a production, tied to research; TH:Cr2.1.Ia, a guide look-for on the same page: one way history or culture changed the student's first idea, with the reason. Each item is marked met, partly or not yet. Out: a team interpretation (Level 2), directors' styles (Level 3). Tools: relationship map, concept page, research note frame, Curio Class's own; Curio Class writes the two root readings. Free path: printed excerpts, pencils. Short path: 17 days | After the bridge because research and concept need script markup and the story map; first of the paired units because every later Level 1 unit uses a researched reading of a play (the character unit's given circumstances, the design unit's concept). Two rows on research and story pair naturally: the roots feed the concept | Candidate: a third storytelling tradition from Asia or Africa beside the Abbey and the ensemble, described and linked | The American Indian Theatre Ensemble: the sensitivity agent reads the company's or a Native-led university program's own pages first; the class studies how the plays were built, and no student performs or imitates a Native story, song or ceremony. Spreading the News is a comedy of gossip; no student is cast to type. Hazards and controls: a clear floor for staging; no real weapons (the play's "murder" is a misunderstanding, nothing is mimed as violence). Agent reads: sensitivity; safety (hands-on). |
| 3 | Level 1 | Inside a character. *How does an actor build a believable person from what the script gives, and how do our own lives shape what we see in a performance?* | 34 | th-g9-actor; th-g9-notice | TH:Pr4.1.Ib; TH:Pr5.1.Ia; TH:Cr3.1.Ib; TH:Cr1.1.Ic; TH:Re7.1.Ia; TH:Re8.1.Ia; TH:Re8.1.Ic | Performing (Pr4, Pr5); creating (Cr1, Cr3); responding (Re7, Re8). Works: Alice Gerstenberg, Overtones (United States, 1913; public domain), a one-act in which two women's hidden selves are played by two more actors, scenes rehearsed; Michael Chekhov's psychological gesture (Russia and the United States, To the Actor, 1953; Chekhov 1891 to 1955), in Curio Class's own summary; Anna Deavere Smith, Twilight: Los Angeles, 1992 (United States, first staged 1993; born 1950, the unit plan records whether living, as of its date), one performer shaping many real people from interviews, described and linked | Retrieval in day 2's warm-up: the warm-up routine and objective, obstacle and tactic (bridge). TH:Pr4.1.Ib: let the given circumstances found in the script (who, where, when, what just happened) guide how the student builds the character. TH:Pr5.1.Ia: work on varied acting techniques (an objective-led run, a psychological gesture, a vocal-pitch exercise) to widen the student's range. TH:Cr3.1.Ib: try body, voice and physical-response choices and keep the most believable. TH:Cr1.1.Ic: keep script notes on the character (wants, fears, what others say of them) as ideas for a believable character. TH:Re7.1.Ia: from what was seen and heard in a scene, draft criteria for judging artists' choices. TH:Re8.1.Ia: compare choices artists made from personal experience across several works (Gerstenberg's hidden selves, Michael Chekhov's gesture, Smith's interviews). TH:Re8.1.Ic: justify the student's own taste and beliefs after taking part in and watching a scene, with a public or invented viewpoint offered beside the student's own and marked the same. Words: given circumstances, objective, tactic, believable, criteria, taste. Showing: a performed character study in pairs. Mid-unit assessment (around unit day 18): the performed character study with its rehearsal log (th-g9-actor). End-of-unit evidence (two check days), id by id: TH:Pr4.1.Ib, a look-for on the character study: choices that follow the given circumstances; TH:Pr5.1.Ia, a kept rehearsal log naming two techniques tried and what each changed; TH:Cr3.1.Ib, a look-for: one body and one voice choice that make the character believable; TH:Cr1.1.Ic, the kept script notes; TH:Re7.1.Ia, a sitting item: criteria drafted from an unseen teacher demonstration; TH:Re8.1.Ia, a sitting item: two artists' choices compared, each tied to the artist's experience; TH:Re8.1.Ic, a kept audience-journal entry justifying a taste after a classmate's showing. Each item is marked met, partly or not yet. Out: a character file with the director's vision (Level 2). Tools: character map, rehearsal log, audience journal, Curio Class's own. Free path: printed scenes. Short path: 17 days | After unit 2 because building a character uses the researched reading of a script; before the design unit because design serves characters already understood. Criteria are formed here, from the student's own response, so unit 4's critique has criteria to use | Candidate: a fourth work from Asia or Africa on how an actor prepares, in a practitioner's own words | Twilight: Los Angeles concerns the 1992 unrest after a police beating: the unit studies how Smith shaped the work, not the events in depth; the sensitivity agent reads the performer's own and a theater's pages first, and no student performs a real person from the play or by an accent. Personal beliefs are invited, never required: a public or invented viewpoint is offered and marked the same. Overtones turns on two women's rivalry over a man: played as written, no student cast by appearance. Health's steps if a student tells something. Agent reads: sensitivity; safety (hands-on). |
| 4 | Level 1 | Design and the critic. *How does a technology change what a designer can do, and how do we judge a play fairly?* | 34 | th-g9-design; th-g9-judge | TH:Cr1.1.Ib; TH:Pr5.1.Ib; TH:Cr3.1.Ic; TH:Re9.1.Ib; TH:Re9.1.Ia; TH:Re9.1.Ic | Creating (Cr1, Cr3); performing (Pr5); responding (Re9). Works: Josef Svoboda's Laterna Magika (Prague, 1958; Svoboda 1920 to 2002), live actors joined with projected film, from the Prague company's or a museum's page; Jean Rosenthal's lighting for dance and Broadway (United States; Rosenthal 1912 to 1969), from a library's archive page; Tomson Highway (Cree, born 1951 in northern Manitoba; the unit plan records whether living, as of its date), The Rez Sisters (Toronto, 1986), seven women of a reserve planning a trip to the biggest bingo in the world, described and linked | Retrieval in day 2's warm-up: the prop sketch and cue sheet (bridge). TH:Cr1.1.Ib: research how one technology (projected film, the electric lighting board) changed design choices. TH:Pr5.1.Ib: use the researched element in a design for a scene of unit 2's or 3's play (a light plot from flashlights and cellophane, or a shadow projection made with a flashlight and a cut card). TH:Cr3.1.Ic: refine the design in a run so it serves the story and its mood. TH:Re9.1.Ib: reflect on how a show's production elements look and feel. TH:Re9.1.Ia: analyze a drama work with evidence and criteria, tied to other arts, history and culture (The Rez Sisters' reserve life, its comedy and the trickster figure in it). TH:Re9.1.Ic: weigh a play's purpose and intended audience to deepen appreciation. Words: design, technology, plot, cue, production element, purpose. Showing: the design shown in a run of the scene for the class. Mid-unit assessment (around unit day 18): the design folio with its short written defense (th-g9-design). End-of-unit evidence (two check days), id by id: TH:Cr1.1.Ib, the kept research page on one technology; TH:Pr5.1.Ib, a look-for on the run: the researched element used in the design; TH:Cr3.1.Ic, a look-for: one change after the critique that strengthens the mood; TH:Re9.1.Ib, a sitting item: how the elements of an unseen described staging look and feel; TH:Re9.1.Ia, the kept written critique of The Rez Sisters with evidence, criteria and one tie to history or another art, drafted, given feedback and revised; TH:Re9.1.Ic, a sitting item: the purpose and intended audience of an unseen scene. Each item is marked met, partly or not yet. Out: a design that carries a concept (Level 2). Tools: cue-sheet practice tool or its printed sheet, design folio, criteria card, Curio Class's own; Curio Class writes the Svoboda and Rosenthal readings. Free path: LED flashlights, cellophane, card. Short path: 17 days | After the character unit because design serves characters and story already studied; the critique uses unit 3's criteria. Before unit 5 because the production needs a design and a habit of fair judgment | Candidate: a living designer from Africa or Latin America, described and linked | The Rez Sisters: the sensitivity agent reads the playwright's own words and a Native-led theater's page first; the play's trickster, Nanabush, is a figure of Cree and Ojibwe story, studied in the playwright's own telling and never performed by students; its scene of sexual violence is not read or staged. Projections: no flashing images. Hazards and controls: LED flashlights only, no light into eyes, no flashing; card cut by an adult with a blade; no work at height; no school stage lights touched by students. Agent reads: sensitivity; safety (hands-on). |
