Each sitting day: set-up 2, the unseen scene 3 to 5, written items 33, collect 3. Every student's evidence is
collected once in a window; no product is scored twice. Days above are band course days (Level 2 starts on day 161,
Level 3 on day 321, Level 4 on day 481). For a senior leaving early, the year-end check moves into their last week
(Grade 12 calendar).

## Cross-subject

No theater row of Levels 1 to 4 carries a cross_subject lean (data/progressions/arts.csv; the grade 8 rows' leans on
ELA end there), so this course waits on no other course and no other course waits on it. A level is not a grade, so
the contacts below are with the student's own ELA and social studies course in whatever grade they are in; all are
core courses (lesson 32). Each is practiced, not owned, with its "if later" line.

### English language arts, grades 9 to 12

| Used here | Contact | If ELA has not taught it yet |
|---|---|---|
| Written critiques (units 4, 9, 14, 19) and dramaturgy pages (units 5, 10) | Claim and evidence writing (W.9-10.1, W.11-12.1) | The unit's critique frame teaches the claim, two pieces of evidence and a reason itself, and the guide marks the theater criteria, not essay form |
| Research notes and source critiques (units 2, 7, 12, 16) | Short research and gathering sources (W.9-10.7, W.9-10.8, W.11-12.7, W.11-12.8) | The research note frame names the source, its maker and date and one check of trust; Curio Class writes the first two readings |
| Every critique day and discussion | Collaborative discussion (SL.9-10.1, SL.11-12.1) | The critique protocol (describe, ask, suggest) carries the turn-taking; the teacher models it on the first critique day |

### Social studies, grades 9 to 12

Works are taught with their time and place (bar criterion 7), using social studies' sourcing habits. The units teach
the setting they use themselves on a one-page setting card (Ireland's revival, Norway in the 1880s, the farm
workers' strike, Cherokee removal); no unit waits on a social studies unit. Sovereignty's history (unit 14) sits
near US History I's removal era, practiced, not owned.

No unit asks for number work beyond counting cues and timing a scene in seconds (lesson 13).

