he same page: a belief system of another place or era and the choice it shaped; TH:Re8.1.Ib, a kept discussion note: two cultural views that could change how the play is judged; TH:Cr2.1.Ib, a kept roles map with each role's dependence named; TH:Cr3.1.Ia, a look-for on the production: staging conventions used and one change from rehearsal; TH:Pr6.1.Ia, a look-for on the production: the scene played for the chosen audience. Each item is marked met, partly or not yet. The production is the unit's showing day; the rehearsal record and roles map are scored after the session. Out: a team interpretation (Level 2). Tools: dramaturgy page, roles map, artist statement, Curio Class's own. Free path: printed scenes, found costume pieces. Short path: 19 days | Last in Level 1 because the production uses every earlier unit (research, character, design, criteria) and ends the level; the artist-statement model starts here, where it is first needed. The year-end check follows in week 32 | Candidate: the production's audience and a family yes for any showing outside class, set in the unit plan | Personal beliefs are invited, never required: a public or invented viewpoint is offered beside the student's own and marked the same. The Lion and the Jewel: the sensitivity agent reads Yoruba-led and Nigerian theater sources first; the play's bride-price plot is studied, not performed, and no student plays a Yoruba character by an accent. Ganashatru concerns a town's poisoned water and religion: the agent reads the film's archive page first. A showing outside class needs the family's yes, or the student's own at 18 or over. Hazards and controls: a clear floor; set pieces at floor level only; no climbing. Agent reads: sensitivity; safety (hands-on). |
