ced to the student's research; TH:Cn11.2.IIb, the kept bias check: one belief that could skew a finding and how the student tested it; TH:Cr1.1.IIa, a look-for on the concept page: two customs that shape the look; TH:Cr2.1.IIa, a look-for: the concept sharpened after feedback, with the change named. Each item is marked met, partly or not yet. Out: blending forms and traditions (Level 3). Tools: research note, bias check card, concept page, Curio Class's own; Curio Class writes the Noh readings. Free path: printed scenes. Short path: 17 days | First of Level 2's paired units, in the same slot as unit 2, because every later Level 2 unit uses the sharpened concept and checked research; review in unit 6 retrieves Level 1's research | Candidate: a Native American or African classic told in its community's own published words, for range | Noh is a living tradition with Buddhist roots: the sensitivity agent reads UNESCO's page and a Noh institution's own pages first; masks are studied by looking only, never made or worn as Noh masks, and no student imitates Noh movement, chant or dance; Okina, a ritual piece, is not used. Agent reads: sensitivity; safety (hands-on). |
