## Findings, most serious first
### 1. Likely phantom duplicate id `MU:Re9.1.Kb` and `MU:Cn10.1`/`Cn11.1` vs official `MU:Cn10.0`/`Cn11.0` in the standards files
### 2. Ownership and handoff rules do not match `scripts/progression.py`, and four gaps block Stage 2
  4. **The Level 1 bridge rule contradicts "assumes no earlier arts learning" and leaves the Grade 8 to Level 1 handoff ambiguous.** Line 103 says "Level 1's first unit assumes no earlier arts learning." Line 110 says "Level 1 opens with a bridge unit of at most 12 days that teaches the discipline's grade 8 ids". Teaching and checking all 15 to 24 grade 8 ids (24 in theater, 21 in dance and music) in 12 days is impossible alongside making, critique, showing and respond, and claiming to "teach" grade 8 ids blurs ownership. `STANDARD.md` also never states whether Grade 8 `hands_on` to Level 1 (`9`) in the progression or how Level 1's `assumes` column handles students entering with no K to 8 arts.
### 3. `APPROACH.md` omits or misplaces half of `UNIT-PLAN.md`'s unit and section fields, and disagrees with the bar on where works and showing live
> - **Unit field 5 (End-of-unit assessment, whole):** The showing or performance guide (three to five look-fors scored met, partly or not yet, never talent, neatness, effort or comparison), or an unseen-work response (visual arts: CC0 image; music: public domain audio or Curio Class playback; media arts: public domain image/clip or Curio Class original; dance and theater: short written description, live teacher demonstration or Curio Class original, never an embed). Two sample descriptions or adult-made examples per open task. Accommodations line: oral or shown response, large-print step card, seated or one-limb version, live observation in place of recording, extra time.
### 4. Cross-subject leans in `STANDARD.md` section 6 name no core progression skill ids
- **What is wrong:** Section 6 lists six cross-subject leans (music/math fractions, visual arts/math geometry and ratio, theater/ELA reading literature and speaking/listening plus claim-and-evidence writing, dance/PE `SHAPE.S1`, media arts/CS privacy and copyright, all arts/social studies sourcing habits). None of the six names a single `skill_id` from `data/progressions/math.csv`, `ela.csv`, `social-studies.csv`, `physical-education.csv` or `computer-science.csv` (only one broad standard strand, `SHAPE.S1`, is given).
### 5. Course list, `core` flags, and high school course count are inconsistent across `STANDARD.md` and `data/courses.csv`
  1. `STANDARD.md` line 5 and `ADOPTION-BAR-SUBJECTS.md` lines 214 and 563 state **20 courses** (five disciplines by four bands: K to 2, 3 to 5, 6 to 8, 9 to 12). `data/courses.csv` still has only **5** rows (`visual-arts`, `music`, `theater`, `dance`, `media-arts`), all with `grades = k-12` (whereas SEL and ELD were updated on 2026-11-21 to four band rows each: `sel-k-2` to `sel-9-12`, `eld-k-2` to `eld-9-12`).
### 6. Dose by band omits high school minutes per lesson and cites no K to 8 national/professional body time figures
     - **EdTA** (Theatre OTL Standards): at least **45 minutes/day for at least one semester each year** in middle school (about 60 to 67.5 hours/year, vs Curio Class's 42.7 hours/year at 64 x 40 min); elementary scheduling comparable to music and visual arts.
  > **Published professional time figures (K to 8).** National arts associations publish Opportunity-to-Learn targets: NAfME recommends at least 90 minutes per week of general music through grade 8; NAEA recommends at least 50 minutes per week in elementary visual arts and grade-level sequential courses in middle school; NDEO recommends 30 minutes per day, 3 to 5 days per week in elementary dance and 40 to 60 minutes per day, 3 to 5 days per week in middle school; EdTA recommends at least 45 minutes per day for one semester each year in middle school theatre. Curio Class's K to 8 default (64 sessions of 40 minutes, 80 minutes per week, about 42.7 hours a year) meets NAEA's elementary floor and sits below the music, dance and middle-school theatre targets; the 32-session short path (40 minutes per week) sits below all four. Each course page names the relevant association figure and the gap so a school can add sessions where its schedule allows.
### 7. Sensitive ground misses four arts-specific risks, has no disclosure/reporting route, and leaves the unit leave-out rule contradictory
- **Cost:** A dance unit could ask a teenager to track an eating plan; a visual arts or theater prompt could elicit an unhandled disclosure of harm; sensitive grades 6 to 12 units could ship without a sensitivity or safety read; and a school leaving out a unit is promised full standards coverage that the unit map cannot keep.
### 8. Stale draft banners, incomplete `Evidence and sources` table, and minor copy faults
  3. `APPROACH.md` line 101 to 102 defines the unseen work on a check day only for dance and theater, leaving visual arts, music and media arts unstated.
