### Handed on to Theater, grades 9 to 12 (Level 1)

Level 1 opens with a bridge unit that teaches the grade 8 techniques and words Level 1 needs, as readiness, and
claims no grade 8 id (STANDARD.md 3.2; th-g9-imagine's note), so a student who did not take this course still enters.

| This course hands on | Unit here | Row that picks it up (Level 1) | What Level 1 does with it |
|---|---|---|---|
| th-g8-imagine | 10 | th-g9-imagine | Research begins a production concept, tested in a concept page |
| th-g8-show | 12 | th-g9-show | The roles of a production are mapped, and a script is staged for an audience the group chooses |
| th-g8-actor | 9 | th-g9-actor | A character is built from the script's given circumstances and tested for believability |
| th-g8-design | 11 | th-g9-design | Research and technology shape the design, defended in a design folio |
| th-g8-story | 10 | th-g9-story | Relationships carry the story, and research finds its cultural and social roots |
| th-g8-world | 12 | th-g9-world | A dramaturgy page compares belief systems across cultures, places and eras |
| th-g8-notice | 9 | th-g9-notice | Criteria are drafted from the student's own response and justified |
| th-g8-judge | 11 | th-g9-judge | A written critique ties a piece to other arts, history and culture |

Each hand-on above is answered at both ends in data/progressions/arts.csv (the th-g9 rows' `assumes` cells).


